# [[after|After]] a [[meal]], especially after [[dinner]].
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Margarita de Veciana|author2=Arthur T. Evans|chapter=Endocrine Disorders: Diabetes|editor=Arthur T. Evans|title=Manual of Obstetrics|edition=7th|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher={{w|Lippincott Williams & Wilkins}}|year=2007|page=285|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=NWKW3KZv9roC&pg=PA285|isbn=978-0-7817-9696-5|passage=Patients who demonstrate significant insulin resistance at diagnosis and require insulin therapy may alternate preprandial and '''postprandial''' evaluations to enable more rapid optimization of control by using an additional supplemental insulin sliding scale as needed to cover preprandial hyperglycaemia (>100 ml per dl){{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Johannes Zschocke|chapter=Function Tests|editors=Georg F. Hoffmann, Johannes Zschocke, and [[w:William Nyhan|William L[eo] Nyhan]]|title=Inherited Metabolic Diseases: A Clinical Approach|location=Heidelberg|publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Springer]]|year=2010|page=347, column 1|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=ILIs-P6sDekC&pg=PA347|doi=10.1007/978-3-540-74723-9|isbn=978-3-540-74722-2|passage=The standardized analysis of metabolic parameters in the preprandial and '''postprandial''' state may provide important functional clues for the diagnosis of metabolic disorders.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|editors=Kathleen P. Freeman and Stefanie Klenner|chapter=Answers|title=Veterinary Clinical Pathology: A Case-based Approach|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher={{w|CRC Press}}|year=2015|page=132|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ei6sCQAAQBAJ&pg=PA132|isbn=978-1-4822-2587-7|passage=The slight increase in pre-prandial and '''post-prandial''' bile acids are suggestive of hepatic dysfunction, but other causes of slight elevations, such as pancreatic or gastrointestinal disease, cannot be ruled out.}}

